## Lessons Learned

### 1. Infrastructure as Code Principles
- Write infrastructure like application code
- Version control everything
- Make changes reviewable (pull requests)
- Document assumptions and dependencies
- Test in non-production first

### 2. Automation Best Practices
- Always handle errors explicitly
- Log everything for debugging
- Test end-to-end without manual steps
- Design for idempotency (safe to run multiple times)
- Graceful degradation (continue on non-critical failures)

### 3. Observability Strategy
- Monitor from day one (not after problems occur)
- Focus on golden signals (latency, traffic, errors, saturation)
- Set alerts for actionable thresholds
- Correlate metrics, logs, and traces
- Make data accessible to team

### 4. Cloud-Specific Lessons
- AWS has constraints (user_data size, IAM permissions)
- Boot sequence is asynchronous (don't assume resources ready)
- Cost optimization matters (t3.micro vs larger instances)
- Regional considerations (latency, compliance)
- State management is critical (Terraform remote state)

